# Revisions `git blame` should look straight through.
#
# Only bulk-formatting commits belong here — a commit that touched a line for a reason
# must never be listed, because in this estate `git blame` is frequently the only record
# of WHY a line is the way it is, and this file is the mechanism that keeps that true
# across a reformat.
#
# --- Turning it on --------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# GitHub's blame view reads this file by itself. Local `git` does not: it is off until a
# clone asks for it, once, with
#
#     git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
#
# and there is no way to ship that setting in the repository, which is why it is written
# here rather than assumed. Without it `git blame` silently attributes every line of the
# reformat below to the reformat.
#
# --- Two properties that decide how entries get added -----------------------------------
#
# A REVISION THAT DOES NOT RESOLVE IS IGNORED IN SILENCE. Measured on git 2.53.0: a
# 40-character SHA naming no object produces no warning, no stderr, and exit 0, and blame
# comes back exactly as if this file were empty. So a wrong SHA here is not a loud
# failure, it is this file quietly not working — verify an entry after adding it, by
# blaming a line the listed commit touched and checking the author is the original one.
#
# THE SHA CANNOT BE ADDED BY THE COMMIT IT NAMES. A commit's SHA is a hash of its own
# content, so there is no fixed point: writing the SHA and amending produces a different
# SHA, and by the property above the file then names a commit that no longer exists and
# fails open. Hence the ordering used below — reformat first, then a second commit, this
# one, carrying the SHA the first one turned out to have.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Item 66, 2026-09-11. Every Java source reformatted with the prettier@3.9.6 /
# prettier-plugin-java@2.10.3 that package-lock.json pins. 31 files. The committed code
# had been matching prettier 3.2.5, the version that was INSTALLED here until item 18 ran
# `npm ci`; `.lintstagedrc.cjs` runs what is installed rather than what is pinned, and no
# CI job looked at formatting at all, so the drift was enforced rather than reported. The
# commit is formatting only and its message lists the two lines in it that are not
# whitespace (Prettier dropping redundant parentheses, both value-preserving).
